ts, it's clear the primary layout should address the empty space in the center, and the secondary row's left-side emptiness. The layout will ideally ensure a balanced and organized structure for both desktop and smaller screen sizes.\n\n\nExploring several layout options for the first row, considering the brand, navigation controls, step indicator, scope badge, and code edit button.  One approach is to center the navigation controls in the first row.\n\nAn alternative puts these elements on the right of the row.  Another approach distributes the control evenly across both rows for better use of space. The second row could display status information or keyboard shortcuts on the left, with preferences on the right.\n\n\nRow one's empty space stems from unbalanced layout choices. Centering the main controls or distributing content across three sections (brand, controls, status) offers improved visual balance. The second row benefits from adding shortcuts on the left and secondary controls on the right to fill empty space. Combining the step counter, scope badge, and central navigation controls also enhances layout balance, providing a more intuitive user interface.\n\n\nExamining the HTML structure reveals that the left-aligned elements in the 'brand-area' combined with right-aligned navigation controls, under `space-between` justify, creates significant empty space. The first row's layout is the main source of the problem.\n\n\nThe secondary top bar's `flex-end` alignment creates a significant empty space to the left, which suggests inefficient layout utilization. The `.three-area-workspace`'s `max-width` constraint and fixed column widths create substantial margins on wider screens and restrict column flexibility. The hard-capped widths of the first and third columns in the grid layout, combined with the overall `max-width`, introduce unnecessary whitespace and reduce adaptability to various screen sizes.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLin